1
00:00:06,919 --> 00:00:16,917
JAMIE: AMD just tanked eight point seven five percent because Wall Street suddenly woke up to the fact that Jensen Huang might actually be irreplaceable, and that scares the living hell out of everyone.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ck1exevgql4qo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

2
00:00:16,917 --> 00:00:41,910
ALEX: S&P down zero point four, Nasdaq off zero point nine one percent, and the VIX climbed three point two one points—which is the kind of move that happens when everyone realizes the same person can't be both the architect of the entire AI infrastructure AND the guy who shows up to your quarterly earnings call. Nvidia itself is down two point zero one percent, but the real damage is upstream: Broadcom cratered four point seven six percent, and Cisco dropped six point two four.

3
00:00:41,910 --> 00:00:48,967
JAMIE: So it's not that the chips stopped working, it's that we stopped working out what happens if Huang gets hit by a bus.

4
00:00:48,967 --> 00:01:03,668
ALEX: Exactly. AMD's down eight point seven five—worse than Broadcom—because the entire sector just got re-priced on succession risk. The market is doing what it does best: pricing in a scenario nobody wants to think about by immediately punishing the stock that benefits most from the status quo staying intact.

5
00:01:03,668 --> 00:01:22,781
MAYA: Meanwhile Meta's up two point nine, Pepsi up two point two four, Coca-Cola up one point eight six—the defensive names that have nothing to do with whether one person wakes up tomorrow are the ones actually moving. It's like everyone suddenly remembered that you can just sell sugar water and snacks to people and nobody needs a single innovation meeting to make that work.

6
00:01:22,781 --> 00:01:30,426
JAMIE: So the AI trade got a reality check, and the market's response was to buy things that don't need a visionary to keep them alive.

7
00:01:30,426 --> 00:01:46,010
MAYA: So here's the thing nobody's saying out loud yet: Nvidia didn't just back AI startups with money. Huang's personal credibility was the collateral. When a Series B founder could say 'Nvidia's betting on us,' that meant something. Now VCs are getting calls from founders asking if the funding window just slammed shut.

8
00:01:46,010 --> 00:01:49,832
ALEX: How much are we talking about in terms of actual capital flow?

9
00:01:49,832 --> 00:02:07,180
MAYA: Nvidia's venture arm has deployed significant capital into the ecosystem—and that's not counting the co-investment gravity it created. When Huang shows up in your cap table, other LPs follow. Without that anchor, founders are looking at smaller checks and VCs are suddenly asking harder questions about unit economics instead of just 'will this be the next big thing.'

10
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:14,531
JAMIE: So it's not that Nvidia stops writing checks tomorrow. It's that the entire confidence mechanism around early-stage AI companies just lost its gravity well.

11
00:02:14,531 --> 00:02:28,645
ALEX: Exactly. Startups are sitting in Series B conversations right now probably getting lower valuations and tighter terms because the person who made it okay to bet on speculative infrastructure plays just disappeared from the equation. That's not a market correction—that's a repricing of risk across the entire cohort.

12
00:02:28,645 --> 00:02:35,408
MAYA: Huang's credibility was the only currency that mattered, and now every founder's wondering if their cap table just got marked to market.

13
00:02:35,408 --> 00:02:40,112
ALEX: The gravity well didn't just collapse—it's still collapsing, and nobody knows where the bottom is.

14
00:02:40,112 --> 00:02:44,229
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
